{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"ENA Express for AWS GovCloud (US) Regions - AWS","context":"AWS has introduced ENA Express, a new network interface for EC2 instances in AWS GovCloud (US) Regions, which utilizes the SRD (scalable reliable datagram) protocol to enhance network performance. ENA Express offers higher single flow bandwidth and lower tail latency for network traffic between EC2 instances, particularly beneficial for workloads like databases, file systems, and distributed storage systems. Previously, customers could use multipath TCP to increase bandwidth, but it adds complexity and may not be compatible with application layers. SRD, a proprietary protocol, delivers these improvements through congestion control, multi-pathing, and packet reordering directly from the Nitro card. Enabling ENA Express is a straightforward configuration, requiring a single command or console toggle for EC2 instances. The SRD protocol increases the maximum single flow bandwidth and improves tail latencies for high throughput workloads, working transparently with TCP and UDP protocols. ENA Express is available in the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions at no additional cost. To learn more and get started, review the latest EC2 documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/06/ena-express-govcloud-us-regions/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"ENA Express for Amazon EC2 instances now supports traffic between Availability Zones - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Network Adapter (ENA) Express now supports traffic between Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (EC2) instances in different Availability Zones within a Region, delivering up to 25 Gbps single-flow bandwidth. ENA Express utilizes the AWS Scalable Reliable Datagram (SRD) protocol for improved network performance, which is also used by Amazon Elastic Block Store (EBS) io2 Block Express and Elastic Fabric Adapter (EFA) for high performance computing and machine learning workloads. ENA Express is available for all supported instance types and sizes in various regions including Africa, Asia Pacific, Canada, Europe, Israel, Mexico, US East, US West, and AWS GovCloud. This feature comes at no additional cost and is designed for workloads such as distributed storage, databases, and file systems that require deployments spanning multiple Availability Zones for resilience. ENA Express establishes an SRD connection when both communicating instances have ENA Express enabled and uses multi-pathing to route traffic across the network, avoiding head-of-line blocking. This feature is available for TCP and UDP protocols and is supported in all regions where ENA Express is available","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/05/ena-express-availability-zones/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"launching-ec2-instance-with-best-practices","skill_description":"Launches an EC2 instance with secure, cost-efficient defaults including AMI selection, burstable instance sizing, least-privilege IAM roles, hardened security groups, encrypted EBS volumes, and comprehensive tagging. Use when deploying new EC2 instances following AWS best practices for security and cost optimization.\n\nServices: ec2, iam, vpc, cloudwatch, ebs\nTasks: deploy, configure, secure\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: compute\n*","skill_name":"launching-ec2-instance-with-best-practices"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"aws-compute","skill_description":"Provisions, scales, and operates Amazon EC2 virtual-machine workloads: instance-type selection (Graviton/Arm64, burstable T credits, GPU, instance store vs EBS), launch templates, Auto Scaling groups (scaling policies, instance refresh, mixed instances, Spot, warm pools, lifecycle hooks), IMDSv2, placement groups, Elastic IPs, AMI lifecycle, and Systems Manager fleet operations (Session Manager, Run Command, Patch Manager). Applies to EC2 instance and fleet questions, InsufficientInstanceCapacity, CPU-credit/surplus charges, IMDSv2 401s, instances stuck in Pending:Wait, ASG not replacing unhealthy instances, status-check failures, SSH refused/timed out, or instances missing as SSM managed nodes. For a single secure instance launch, the launching-ec2-instance-with-best-practices skill is more appropriate; for instance profiles, see setting-up-ec2-instance-profiles; for Image Builder, see amazon-ec2-image-builder. Does NOT cover Lambda, ECS/Fargate, EKS, VPC/ALB/NLB design, or IAM policy authoring.\n\nServices: ec2, autoscaling, ssm, elasticloadbalancing\nTasks: provision, scale, operate, debug, optimize\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: compute\n*","skill_name":"aws-compute"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-network-monitoring","skill_description":"Installs, configures, and troubleshoots Network Flow Monitor agents on EC2 instances to monitor network path health. Covers agent installation, IAM permissions, monitoring network paths, and troubleshooting agents reporting no metrics, HTTP 403 errors, or connectivity failures.\n\nServices: network-flow-monitor, ec2, ssm, cloudwatch\nTasks: deploy, debug\nPersona: developer, devops, network-engineer\nWorkload: networking\n*","skill_name":"aws-network-monitoring"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify AWS EC2 ENA TCP segmentation offload behavior explains oversized tcpdump segments"}
